sections.content:
Na pitanje o obiteljskoj molitvi Politeo kaže da su on i Antonija od početka braka tražili zajedničko vrijeme za molitvu i isprobali više varijanti. Ustalio se trenutak polaganja djece na spavanje, a uveli su ga nakon rođenja sina **Tome**.

Večernji ritual izgleda ovako:

- između 20 i 21 sat svi se okupe na roditeljskom krevetu;
- djeca izmole **Anđele čuvaru** i zahvale za dan, pri čemu ih roditelji potiču da promisle i ne ponavljaju svaki dan isto;
- slijede puse i križići, a djeca odlaze u krevet, jer svi spavaju u istoj sobi;
- supružnici mole **Gospinu krunicu** i zahvale, a djeca uz to najčešće zaspu.

Dok je Toma bio sam, češće je molio s njima. Sada se djeca više zaigraju, a roditelji im molitvu ne žele nametati. Djeca ipak znaju da se krunica mora moliti. Politeo se prisjeća jedne prilike na moru kada su djeca sama pitala: „Pa kud bez krunice?“

Supružnici imaju i osobne molitve te još jednu zajedničku, ujutro ili kasno navečer. Ponekad dan krene neočekivano, primjerice kad netko razbije usnicu, slijedi hitna, a djeca ne idu u vrtić, pa se svi planovi pomaknu. „Sve je to kako treba bit“, kaže.

sections.content_en:
When asked about family prayer, Politeo says that he and Antonija sought a shared time for prayer from the beginning of their marriage and tried several variations. The moment of putting the children to sleep became established, and they introduced it after the birth of their son **Toma**.

The evening ritual looks like this:

- between 8:00 and 9:00 PM everyone gathers on the parents' bed;
- the children pray the **Guardian Angel** and thanksgivings for the day, with the parents encouraging them to reflect and not repeat the same thing every day;
- kisses and little crosses follow, and the children go to bed, because everyone sleeps in the same room;
- the spouses pray **Our Lady's Rosary** and thanksgivings, and the children usually fall asleep to it.

While Toma was an only child, he prayed with them more often. Now the children get more carried away playing, and the parents do not want to impose prayer on them. The children nevertheless know that the Rosary must be prayed. Politeo recalls an occasion at the seaside when the children themselves asked: „Well, where without the Rosary?“

The spouses also have personal prayers and another shared one, in the morning or late in the evening. Sometimes the day starts unexpectedly, for example when someone cuts a lip, the emergency room follows, and the children do not go to kindergarten, so all plans shift. „It's all as it should be,“ he says.
